LOK: a snapshot gives way while its zip is written

A snapshot (patch 0090) asked for waiting input while the filter
wrote its XML parts, then never again: the package commit that
follows copies every stream into the zip. On a deck of pictures that
is the longer half; a key pressed in it waited the rest out (Ely
R18.8: 105 ms of a 385 ms snapshot of 189 pictures, 152 ms on a
slower machine).
The commit now asks too: before each entry, at each 32 KB copied
into the zip, at each batch of a large stream's deflate, and while
it waits for the deflating threads. finish() polls for those threads
as reduceScheduledThreadTasksToGivenNumberOrLess always did, so the
wait can ask.
A zip stream abandoned with entries still queued leaked them, their
buffer files and their running tasks; any failed entry did that, a
stop now does it often. The destructor waits for the tasks and
frees the entries, and an entry leaves the queue before its copy
may throw. A deflating task ends at its next 32 KB once the
snapshot has stopped, so that wait is short; the stopped flag is
atomic for it.
